WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.250
When we take the bus to work, changing road conditions can make us late.

00:00:08.583 --> 00:00:16.833
A digital twin system can use sensor data to help the bus choose a route.

00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:25.417
But choosing a route before the next junction is only part of the challenge.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:34.000
If the digital twin system has not received the latest road conditions,

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:42.583
even an on-time instruction can be unhelpful or lead to costly mistakes.

00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:53.167
Could we solve this by updating every digital state immediately?

00:00:53.500 --> 00:01:03.750
At scale, these updates compete for limited bandwidth.

00:01:04.083 --> 00:01:14.333
Trying to send everything can leave important changes waiting.

00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:22.667
The key is that different updates have different value for each task.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Age and frequency of change offer clues, but do not capture the full picture.

00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:39.583
So we need to prioritize the updates that matter to the decision.

00:01:39.917 --> 00:01:48.167
Alongside these updates, digital twin systems run many computational tasks.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:02:00.208
These tasks are distributed across edge and cloud resources.

00:02:00.542 --> 00:02:12.250
With limited resources and tight deadlines, deciding what runs where becomes difficult.

00:02:12.583 --> 00:02:24.292
Task execution and state updates must work together to meet diverse requirements.

00:02:24.625 --> 00:02:32.875
To address these challenges, we study scheduling through modeling and theoretical optimization.

00:02:33.208 --> 00:02:41.458
We then develop and evaluate methods for task execution and state synchronization.

00:02:41.792 --> 00:02:50.042
We will also explore agentic methods that adapt scheduling as conditions change.

00:02:50.375 --> 00:02:58.375
Across these settings, scheduling objectives and constraints vary widely.

00:02:58.708 --> 00:03:06.708
No single scheduling method is best for every combination of requirements.

00:03:07.042 --> 00:03:15.292
This also motivates us to explore how AI can generate scheduling algorithms.

00:03:15.625 --> 00:03:23.625
These AI-generated candidates must be tested against constraints and performance goals.

00:03:23.958 --> 00:03:32.208
Together, these efforts help digital twin systems turn data into timely, useful decisions.

00:03:32.542 --> 00:03:40.792
Our goal is to build better digital twin systems that improve everyday life.